            five-year column is simple arithmetic on today's price and assumes no increase,
            which history suggests is optimistic. Peloton, for example, moved All-Access from
            $44 to $49.99 in October 2025.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two things stand out. First, the spread is wide: over five years, Peloton paid
            monthly costs about four times as much as JRNY All-Access paid yearly. Second, the yearly
            plans are where most of the saving is. Paying iFIT Pro yearly rather than monthly
            saves about $72 a year, and Echelon's yearly plan saves about $80 against twelve
            monthly payments.
          </p>
          <p>
            The column that matters for this page, though, is the last row. Sole's companion
            app adds workouts and tracking with no monthly fee, which is why the five-year cost
            of a Sole is its purchase price. If you are weighing iFIT in particular, our <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">breakdown of whether iFIT is worth it</a> looks at the Train and Pro tiers on their own terms, and <a href="/ifit-vs-peloton/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT versus Peloton</a> compares the two biggest libraries head to head. If you want Peloton&rsquo;s classes without
            its hardware, our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-peloton-app/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for the Peloton App</a> pairs subscription-free machines with App+.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-you-lose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What You Actually Lose Without One</h2>
<p>
            Going by manufacturer documentation for every machine here, the
            losses with nothing paid fall into four categories.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Guided classes and scenic runs.</strong> Always lost, on every machine. This
            is what the subscription is for and nobody pretends otherwise. Whether that matters
            depends entirely on whether you would use them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Automatic incline and speed control.</strong> Often lost, and this one
            surprises people. On several machines the trainer-controlled adjustment that changes
            the incline for you is a subscription feature, and without it you set everything by
            hand. The hardware is identical; the automation is rented.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Built-in programs.</strong> Varies enormously. Some machines keep a dozen
            onboard workouts that need no connection at all; others have none, because the
            manufacturer expected the subscription to provide them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Workout history and metrics.</strong> Frequently lost or reduced to a single
            session's numbers with no record. If you care about tracking mileage over months, check
            whether the machine exports to a third-party app over Bluetooth, because several do and
            it removes the problem entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="nordictrack-proform-without-ifit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which NordicTrack and ProForm Models Work Without iFIT</h2>
<p>
            This is the most common version of the question, because NordicTrack and ProForm
            are the connected treadmills most US buyers meet first, in stores and online. The short answer is that
            every current model runs without a paid iFIT membership, and none of them is a
            no-subscription treadmill in the Sole sense. What differs is how much of the machine
            you paid for still earns its keep.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What the brands say.</strong> NordicTrack's iFIT FAQ says manual mode is
            always available, the membership is not mandatory, and if you do not renew you keep
            a limited number of iFIT workouts. ProForm's current product pages go further on
            setup: they state that a free iFIT account, agreement to terms, Wi-Fi and a credit
            card are required to activate the equipment, even though no charge is made for the
            account itself. A free account is not a paid membership, but it does mean the
            machine is not anonymous, and it is worth knowing before delivery day.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How the range splits.</strong> Roughly, the models fall into three groups:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Touchscreen models</strong> (the Commercial series, the X-series incline
            trainers, the ProForm Pro and Carbon Pro lines). Manual speed and incline work
            unpaid, but the large screen, the automatic trainer control and most of the workout
            content live in iFIT. These are the machines where the most money sits idle without
            a membership.</li>
            <li><strong>Bring-your-own-device models</strong> (the NordicTrack T Series 5 and
            similar). A plain LCD runs the belt; iFIT, if you want it, runs on your own phone or
            tablet with the cheaper Train membership. Unpaid, these are simply basic treadmills,
            which is a fair description of what you paid for.</li>
            <li><strong>Older button-console machines.</strong> Many earlier NordicTrack and
            ProForm models stored a set of hill and interval programs on the console that run
            with no account at all. That is the source of a lot of out-of-date advice: the
            current touchscreen consoles do not have an equivalent bank of onboard programs.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The practical rule: if you already know you will never pay for iFIT, the
            bring-your-own-device models are the only ones in the two ranges where you are not
            paying for hardware you will not use. Our full answer to <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether you can use a NordicTrack without iFIT</a> walks through what the screen does unpaid, the activation question and what it
            means at resale, and <a href="/nordictrack-vs-proform/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack versus ProForm</a> compares the two sister brands model for model.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose</h2>
<p>
            We compared each machine on what its manufacturer documents about how it behaves with the subscription cancelled or never activated: manual speed
            and incline, onboard programs, metric display, history, Bluetooth pairing to a
            third-party app, and whether the machine prompts for payment during ordinary use.
          </p>
          <p>
            That last point matters more than it sounds. A machine can be technically fully
            functional without a subscription yet prompt for one so persistently — on
            wake, before a run, after a run — that using them becomes a mildly unpleasant experience.
            Functionality and usability are different things, and a machine that works but nags is
            not the same as one that simply works.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also looked at whether the manufacturer requires an account to complete initial
            setup, which several do even where no payment is needed. That is worth knowing if
            you would rather the machine were not connected to anything, and it also affects
            resale, because the next owner inherits the account question.
          </p>
        </div>

